{"term":"ladder","senses":[{"id":"kaikki:65e80504c9fcacbc2e","definition":"A frame, usually portable, of wood, metal, or rope, used for ascent and descent, consisting of two side pieces to which are fastened rungs (cross strips or rounds acting as steps).","example":"The form of a man was seen to enter, and both the females rushed up the ladder, as if equally afraid of the consequences. The stranger secured the door, and first examining the lower room with great care, he cautiously ascended the ladder.","pos":"noun","synonyms":["stepladder"],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"","register":"","wordFamily":["accommodation ladder","aerial ladder","angel's ladder","Appalachian Jacob's ladder","attic ladder","Cantril ladder","chain-ladder method","chicken ladder","chutes and ladders","climb the ladder","climb up the ladder","coin ladder","companion ladder","corporate ladder","crossed ladders problem","DNA ladder","downladder","fail up the ladder","fare ladder","fish ladder","hook ladder","interladder","Jacob's ladder","ladderane","ladder back","ladderback","ladder-back","ladder beam","laddered","laddergram"],"source":"English Wiktionary via Kaikki.org","attribution":"English Wiktionary contributors","sourceUrl":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ladder#English","license":"CC-BY-SA-4.0"},{"id":"kaikki:942a19edeff362d018","definition":"A series of stages by which one progresses to a better position.","example":"Newcastle had won both their previous fixtures in 2011 but were terribly disappointing at Broadhall Way against opponents 73 places below them in the footballing ladder.","pos":"noun","synonyms":[],"antonyms":[],"collocations":[],"usage":"figuratively","register":"figuratively","wordFamily":["accommodation ladder","aerial ladder","angel's ladder","Appalachian Jacob's ladder","attic ladder","Cantril ladder","chain-ladder method","chicken ladder","chutes and ladders","climb the ladder","climb up the ladder","coin ladder","companion ladder","corporate ladder","cross